        "action": "fill",
        "value": "I'm an Applied Mathematics & Statistics and Economics undergraduate at Johns Hopkins, and most of my recent work has been on the modeling side of noisy event data. At Summer Atlantic Capital / SAC Nexus I build the machine-learning core of an anomaly-detection and alert-prioritization system: an event-ingestion pipeline, temporal sequence models over event streams using RNN and Transformer-style approaches, anomaly scoring, and a learning-to-rank layer that decides which alerts an analyst sees first. That last piece is the part I care about most. A detector that fires constantly creates a second problem instead of solving the first, so ranking and explanation quality matter as much as raw model performance. I built the serving side too, a versioned FastAPI microservice with CI scaffolding, because a signal nobody can run reliably is not a signal yet. Earlier, on a Gates Foundation-funded Hopkins project, I trained XGBoost models on roughly 10 million survey records and used SHAP-based feature selection with the principal investigator to decide what actually belonged in the specification. Internal Alpha Capture sounds like the same loop, research, ML, and real data infrastructure, applied to equity signals, and that is the work I want to be doing.",
